  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations to New Jersey reached $5.4B in FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 42.0% year over year. Those obligations were recorded across 304,337 awards, with an average award size of $17.8K. Recent fiscal-year history shows higher annual totals: $8.9B in FY21, $9.3B in FY22, $9.2B in FY23, $9.8B in FY24, and $9.3B in FY25. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, accounting for $3.3B of FY2026 to date obligations, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $602M and the Department of Homeland Security at $553M. Top vendors include LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $1.1B and several large contractors between $423M and $49.7M. Leading industries by obligations are aerospace and defense manufacturing at $751M, physical and engineering research at $673M, and commercial and institutional building construction at $483M. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial, reflecting fiscal year to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in New Jersey?",
        "body": "Buying is concentrated. The Department of Defense accounts for $3.3B of the $5.4B total in FY2026 to date. The next two agencies, the Department of Veterans Affairs and the Department of Homeland Security, account for $602M and $553M respectively. That concentration is also visible in vendors, where LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION holds $1.1B in obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What changed compared with prior years?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 42.0% year over year in FY2026 to date. Annual obligations in the prior five fiscal years ranged from $8.9B to $9.8B, while FY26 is $5.4B so far. The drop shows a sizable reduction in current-year obligations compared with the recent annual totals. Award count and average award size remain recorded at 304,337 awards and $17.8K respectively."
      }
    ]
